  "rule": {
    "title": "Present perfect vs past simple",
    "explanation": "El present perfect conecta el pasado con el presente:\n- experiencias sin fecha: «Have you ever managed a team?»\n- periodos que siguen abiertos: «I've worked here for six months.» (y sigo)\n- resultados que importan ahora: «I've lost my keys.» (no las tengo)\n- con «today», «this week», «this year» si aún no han terminado: «I've had three meetings today.»\n\nEl past simple habla de un momento o periodo terminado:\n- «In Madrid I managed three designers.»\n- «I left in August.»\n- «I worked there for two years.» (ya no trabajo allí)\n\nLas preguntas con «When…?» y las frases con «yesterday», «last…», «… ago» o una fecha van siempre en past simple:\n- «When did you finish that job?»\n- «I started two years ago.»\n\nEn una conversación es normal empezar con present perfect y pasar al past simple para los detalles:\n- «Have you ever been to Japan? — Yes, I have. I went in 2019. It was amazing.»",
    "table": {
      "headers": [
        "Present perfect",
        "Past simple"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Have you ever…?",
          "When did you…?"
        ],
        [
          "I've worked here for a year. (sigo)",
          "I worked there for a year. (ya no)"
        ],
        [
          "this week, today, ever, never, yet",
          "yesterday, last week, ago, in 2019"
        ],
        [
          "I've lost my keys. (ahora no las tengo)",
          "I lost my keys last night."
        ]
      ]
    }
  },
